      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hereford was founded as \"Blue Water\" in 1899 when the Pecos and Northern Texas Railway linked Amarillo to Farwell.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hereford%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Residents renamed the town Hereford after the cattle brought by ranchers, honoring the Hereford breed that arrived in 1898.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        5,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hereford-tx",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The National Cowgirl Hall of Fame opened in Hereford in 1919 before relocating to Fort Worth in 1994.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/hereford-tx",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Radio station KPAN first broadcast from Hereford in August 1948, becoming a local voice for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
